1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,894
Imagine stepping outside and feeling your eyelashes freeze together

2
00:00:04,894 --> 00:00:08,473
in seconds. Your breath crackles in the air. Your

3
00:00:08,473 --> 00:00:12,199
phone dies. The ground is frozen hundreds of meters

4
00:00:12,199 --> 00:00:16,070
deep. This isn't Antarctica. It's a town where people

5
00:00:16,070 --> 00:00:19,649
live, go to school and raise families. Welcome to

6
00:00:19,649 --> 00:00:23,083
Oymyakon, the coldest inhabited place on Earth.

7
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:27,167
Welcome to Earth Decoded. If you love the most

8
00:00:27,167 --> 00:00:31,546
extreme places on our planet, tap subscribe. Today, we're

9
00:00:31,546 --> 00:00:34,235
visiting the coldest town on Earth.

10
00:00:34,785 --> 00:00:38,721
Oymyakon is a small village in the Sakha Republic,

11
00:00:38,721 --> 00:00:42,500
also called Yakutia, in the far east of Siberia,

12
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:45,964
Russia. About five hundred people live here.

13
00:00:46,514 --> 00:00:50,768
In February 1933, the temperature here was recorded at

14
00:00:50,768 --> 00:00:55,890
minus sixty-seven point seven degrees Celsius. That's the coldest

15
00:00:55,890 --> 00:01:01,405
temperature ever officially recorded in a permanently inhabited place.

16
00:01:01,955 --> 00:01:05,581
Oymyakon has a rival. The town of Verkhoyansk, a

17
00:01:05,581 --> 00:01:10,341
few hundred kilometers away, claims a similar record. Together,

18
00:01:10,341 --> 00:01:13,212
the two towns are known as the Pole of

19
00:01:13,212 --> 00:01:15,629
Cold of the Northern Hemisphere.

20
00:01:16,179 --> 00:01:20,465
In January, the average temperature is around minus fifty

21
00:01:20,465 --> 00:01:23,774
degrees. Not the coldest night. The average.

22
00:01:24,324 --> 00:01:27,735
Funny enough, the name Oymyakon is often said to

23
00:01:27,735 --> 00:01:31,431
mean unfrozen water, because of a natural hot spring

24
00:01:31,431 --> 00:01:35,481
nearby where reindeer herders once watered their animals.

25
00:01:36,031 --> 00:01:38,786
Why is it so cold? Oymyakon sits in a

26
00:01:38,786 --> 00:01:42,808
valley surrounded by mountains, far from any ocean. On

27
00:01:42,808 --> 00:01:46,903
long, clear winter nights, heat escapes into space, and

28
00:01:46,903 --> 00:01:50,701
the heaviest, coldest air sinks into the valley and

29
00:01:50,701 --> 00:01:51,669
gets trapped.

30
00:01:52,219 --> 00:01:55,861
In December, the sun shines for only about three

31
00:01:55,861 --> 00:01:59,579
hours a day. In summer, it's the opposite: almost

32
00:01:59,579 --> 00:02:04,284
endless daylight, and temperatures can even climb above thirty

33
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:04,891
degrees.

34
00:02:05,441 --> 00:02:09,937
Quick question. What's the coldest temperature you've ever experienced?

35
00:02:09,937 --> 00:02:11,457
Tell me in the comments.

36
00:02:12,007 --> 00:02:15,154
So what happens to your body at minus fifty?

37
00:02:15,154 --> 00:02:18,301
Exposed skin can get frostbite in just a few

38
00:02:18,301 --> 00:02:21,735
minutes. Locals cover every bit of skin with fur

39
00:02:21,735 --> 00:02:25,383
hats, scarves and thick boots made of reindeer fur.

40
00:02:25,933 --> 00:02:30,101
The trick is layers. Wool underneath, then thick sweaters,

41
00:02:30,101 --> 00:02:33,335
then a long fur coat. Many locals say natural

42
00:02:33,335 --> 00:02:37,288
fur and reindeer-skin boots work better than any modern

43
00:02:37,288 --> 00:02:39,373
jacket at these temperatures.

44
00:02:39,923 --> 00:02:43,542
Your eyelashes and eyebrows turn white with frost. If

45
00:02:43,542 --> 00:02:46,615
you wear glasses, the metal can stick to your

46
00:02:46,615 --> 00:02:50,166
skin. Breathing that air hurts your lungs, so people

47
00:02:50,166 --> 00:02:51,805
breathe through scarves.

48
00:02:52,355 --> 00:02:57,132
At these temperatures, your breath freezes almost instantly, making

49
00:02:57,132 --> 00:03:00,697
a soft crackling sound. Yakut people have a poetic

50
00:03:00,697 --> 00:03:03,406
name for it: the whisper of the stars.

51
00:03:03,956 --> 00:03:07,407
If you got lost outside in winter without proper

52
00:03:07,407 --> 00:03:11,504
clothing, hypothermia could set in within an hour. That's

53
00:03:11,504 --> 00:03:14,452
why nobody here goes far alone in winter.

54
00:03:15,002 --> 00:03:19,487
Machines struggle too. Car batteries die, oil turns thick

55
00:03:19,487 --> 00:03:23,658
like honey, and plastic snaps. Many people keep their

56
00:03:23,658 --> 00:03:27,278
car engines running all day, or park in heated

57
00:03:27,278 --> 00:03:30,347
garages. If a car stops on the road, it

58
00:03:30,347 --> 00:03:31,685
can freeze solid.

59
00:03:32,235 --> 00:03:35,862
Houses are built for war against the cold. Thick

60
00:03:35,862 --> 00:03:40,093
wooden walls, small double or triple windows, and stoves

61
00:03:40,093 --> 00:03:43,947
burning coal or wood almost nonstop through winter.

62
00:03:44,497 --> 00:03:48,022
If the heating fails in midwinter, a house can

63
00:03:48,022 --> 00:03:52,238
become dangerously cold within hours, and the pipes can

64
00:03:52,238 --> 00:03:55,763
freeze and burst. So keeping the fire going is

65
00:03:55,763 --> 00:03:56,913
everyone's job.

66
00:03:57,463 --> 00:04:01,222
Phone batteries drain in minutes, so people keep them

67
00:04:01,222 --> 00:04:04,981
inside their coats. Pens stop writing because the ink

68
00:04:04,981 --> 00:04:05,548
freezes.

69
00:04:06,098 --> 00:04:10,126
Under the town is permafrost, ground that has stayed

70
00:04:10,126 --> 00:04:13,844
frozen for thousands of years. It's so hard that

71
00:04:13,844 --> 00:04:17,484
pipes are difficult to bury, so many homes have

72
00:04:17,484 --> 00:04:21,512
outdoor toilets, a wooden outhouse in the yard, even

73
00:04:21,512 --> 00:04:22,674
at minus fifty.

74
00:04:23,224 --> 00:04:27,534
The permafrost is also a time capsule. Across Yakutia,

75
00:04:27,534 --> 00:04:32,083
scientists have found woolly mammoths, cave lion cubs and

76
00:04:32,083 --> 00:04:36,393
ancient horses, frozen for tens of thousands of years,

77
00:04:36,393 --> 00:04:39,267
some with fur and skin still intact.

78
00:04:39,817 --> 00:04:43,024
Even burials are hard. To dig a grave, people

79
00:04:43,024 --> 00:04:46,659
traditionally light fires on the ground to thaw it,

80
00:04:46,659 --> 00:04:49,225
little by little, over several days.

81
00:04:49,775 --> 00:04:53,301
Children still go to school in winter. Classes are

82
00:04:53,301 --> 00:04:57,039
usually only canceled when it drops below about minus

83
00:04:57,039 --> 00:04:58,308
fifty-two degrees.

84
00:04:58,858 --> 00:05:02,865
Food is mostly meat and fish, because almost nothing

85
00:05:02,865 --> 00:05:07,104
grows here. A local favorite is stroganina, thin slices

86
00:05:07,104 --> 00:05:10,802
of raw frozen fish. Reindeer and horse meat give

87
00:05:10,802 --> 00:05:13,962
people the energy they need to stay warm.

88
00:05:14,512 --> 00:05:17,712
Would you try stroganina? Tell me in the comments.

89
00:05:18,262 --> 00:05:21,976
The animals here are tough too. Yakutian horses live

90
00:05:21,976 --> 00:05:25,762
outside all winter. They grow thick, shaggy coats and

91
00:05:25,762 --> 00:05:29,334
can find grass under the snow at temperatures that

92
00:05:29,334 --> 00:05:30,977
would kill most horses.

93
00:05:31,527 --> 00:05:35,007
Some locals even enjoy the cold. After a hot

94
00:05:35,007 --> 00:05:38,646
steam bath, called a banya, a few brave people

95
00:05:38,646 --> 00:05:41,731
jump into the snow or into holes cut in

96
00:05:41,731 --> 00:05:42,364
the ice.

97
00:05:42,914 --> 00:05:46,177
The Yakut people, or Sakha, have lived in this

98
00:05:46,177 --> 00:05:50,434
region for centuries, herding horses and reindeer, and their

99
00:05:50,434 --> 00:05:53,697
knowledge of the cold is the reason anyone can

100
00:05:53,697 --> 00:05:54,903
live here at all.

101
00:05:55,453 --> 00:05:58,706
Getting here isn't easy. The main road is the

102
00:05:58,706 --> 00:06:02,391
Kolyma Highway, nicknamed the Road of Bones. It was

103
00:06:02,391 --> 00:06:05,788
built in Soviet times by prisoners of the Gulag

104
00:06:05,788 --> 00:06:09,330
labor camps, and many of them died while building

105
00:06:09,330 --> 00:06:09,619
it.

106
00:06:10,169 --> 00:06:13,869
Today, drivers cross it in convoys in winter, because

107
00:06:13,869 --> 00:06:17,081
a breakdown alone on the road could be deadly.

108
00:06:17,631 --> 00:06:21,688
The regional capital, Yakutsk, has more than three hundred

109
00:06:21,688 --> 00:06:25,536
thousand people. It's often called the coldest big city

110
00:06:25,536 --> 00:06:28,894
in the world, and it's built on permafrost, with

111
00:06:28,894 --> 00:06:31,903
many buildings standing on concrete stilts.

112
00:06:32,453 --> 00:06:36,082
Why stilts? If buildings sat directly on the ground,

113
00:06:36,082 --> 00:06:39,711
their heat would melt the permafrost, and they would

114
00:06:39,711 --> 00:06:42,992
sink and crack. Raising them lets cold air flow

115
00:06:42,992 --> 00:06:43,759
underneath.

116
00:06:44,309 --> 00:06:48,189
But the permafrost is changing. As the Arctic warms,

117
00:06:48,189 --> 00:06:52,516
it's thawing in many places across Siberia, damaging roads

118
00:06:52,516 --> 00:06:57,441
and buildings and releasing greenhouse gases trapped for thousands

119
00:06:57,441 --> 00:06:58,112
of years.

120
00:06:58,662 --> 00:07:02,252
Not far from Yakutsk stand the Lena Pillars, rock

121
00:07:02,252 --> 00:07:05,768
towers up to about one hundred meters tall along

122
00:07:05,768 --> 00:07:09,284
the Lena River, a UNESCO World Heritage Site and

123
00:07:09,284 --> 00:07:12,507
one of the most beautiful places in Siberia.

124
00:07:13,057 --> 00:07:17,003
Every year, Oymyakon hosts a winter festival called the

125
00:07:17,003 --> 00:07:20,949
Pole of Cold, with reindeer races, traditional food and

126
00:07:20,949 --> 00:07:24,321
visitors who come just to say they survived it.

127
00:07:24,871 --> 00:07:28,643
Each winter, a few brave tourists travel to Oymyakon

128
00:07:28,643 --> 00:07:31,835
just to stand next to the town's famous sign

129
00:07:31,835 --> 00:07:33,575
marking the record cold.

130
00:07:34,125 --> 00:07:37,821
So, could you survive Oymyakon? On your own, without

131
00:07:37,821 --> 00:07:41,517
the right clothes, a warm house and local knowledge,

132
00:07:41,517 --> 00:07:44,716
maybe not for long. But hundreds of people do

133
00:07:44,716 --> 00:07:47,914
it every year, and many of them wouldn't live

134
00:07:47,914 --> 00:07:48,909
anywhere else.

135
00:07:49,459 --> 00:07:53,428
Last question. Which extreme place should we explore next?

136
00:07:53,428 --> 00:07:55,070
Tell me in the comments.

137
00:07:55,620 --> 00:07:59,373
Thanks for watching Earth Decoded. If you enjoyed this,

138
00:07:59,373 --> 00:08:02,512
like the video, subscribe, and share it with a

139
00:08:02,512 --> 00:08:05,720
friend who always says they're cold. See you in

140
